BODY = '''<h2>What MedlinePlus and the Clinics Say</h2>
<p>MedlinePlus, in its "Bathroom safety for adults" page (reviewed April 2026), advises you to sit on a bath chair or bench when taking a shower. It also suggests non-slip suction mats or rubber silicone decals in the tub, a non-skid mat outside it, and two grab bars: one to help you get in and out of the tub and one to help you stand, fixed vertically or horizontally and not diagonally.</p>
<p>For the steps, a hospital rehabilitation handout from the Greater Baltimore Center for Rehabilitation Medicine, the Canadian Red Cross and Health New Zealand agree on the order. You back up until the bench touches your legs, sit on the outside end, then lift your legs over the wall, and you reverse it to get out. We set it out in our <a href="/articles/how-to-use-a-tub-transfer-bench/">how-to article</a>.</p>
<p class="note"><strong>A bench is not a prescription.</strong> A doctor or occupational therapist can watch you transfer and say whether a bench suits you and how high to set it.</p>

<h2>How to Choose a Tub Transfer Bench</h2>
<h3>Weight limit</h3>
<p>The maker pages and catalog entries we found list 400 pounds for all five. Two things to watch: Forbes Health listed the Vive at 350 pounds in 2024 where Vive's page says 400, and our October check of the Drive's Amazon listing said 350 where Drive's page says 400. When two figures disagree, use the lower one and read the label on the bench you receive. Health New Zealand notes that most transfer benches have a maximum weight capacity and that you should check it.</p>
<h3>Will it fit your tub</h3>
<p>Compare the bench to the tub wall height and the floor space. Vive says its bench clears a wall up to 21 inches and has a 30.5 by 22 inch base. Drive's seat is 26 inches wide and 18.5 to 19.5 inches deep. The Red Cross says the legs must reach the floor and the bench should not rest on the tub wall. None of the sources we read gives a rule for tub width, so ask a therapist if your tub is unusual.</p>
<h3>Height range</h3>
<p>Drive and Carex list 17.5 to 22.5 inches, Medline 18 to 22.5 and Vive 18.5 to 23.5. The inside and outside legs need different heights because most tub floors are higher than the bathroom floor, and the Red Cross says to set each pair level so the bench ends up approximately level.</p>
<h3>Back and arm</h3>
<p>All five have a back and a side arm. Carex, Vive and Medline say the back reverses, and Drive calls its bench reversible, which lets you set the arm on the side you need.</p>
<h3>Feet</h3>
<p>Vive and the padded Medline list suction cups inside the tub and rubber outside. Drive sells suction cups separately. Health New Zealand says to check that suction cups hold the bench firmly, and the Red Cross advises a slip-resistant mat in the tub.</p>
<h3>Padding</h3>
<p>Only the padded Medline has it. The Red Cross says to wipe a transfer bench dry after every use and to wash it with mild soapy water.</p>

<h2>Tub Transfer Bench Questions</h2>
<h3>Do I need a transfer bench or a shower chair?</h3>
<p>If you can step into the tub safely, a shower chair works. A bench is for a tub you cannot step into. See <a href="/best-shower-chairs/">our shower chair guide</a> and the <a href="/articles/shower-chair-or-transfer-bench/">comparison article</a>.</p>
<h3>Can I use one in a shower?</h3>
<p>Health New Zealand describes using a transfer bench in a shower as well as a bath, and says you can remove the backrest in a shower if you do not need it, and that you need room for your legs.</p>
<h3>Does insurance pay for one?</h3>
<p>It depends on the plan. See <a href="/articles/does-medicare-pay-for-mobility-equipment/">what Medicare pays for</a>.</p>
<h3>Do I still need grab bars?</h3>
<p>Yes. MedlinePlus recommends two. See <a href="/articles/where-to-put-grab-bars/">where to put grab bars</a>.</p>
<h3>Do I need someone with me?</h3>
<p>The Red Cross recommends supervision or assistance for users with limited mobility.</p>

<h2>How We Compare to Other Lists</h2>
<p>Forbes Health's list of tub transfer benches, audited in April 2024, names three benches, and Healthline's shower chair list, updated in 2022, names three benches among its ten seats. Their entries give a price, a weight limit and a height range, but not how tall a tub wall the bench clears or the size of the seat. This guide is dated 2026, flags where figures disagree and says which pages we could not read.</p>

<h2>Our Take</h2>
<p>The Drive 12011KD-1 is the best all-round choice, for its large seat, the complete figures on the maker's page and its warranty, with the caveat that suction cups are sold separately. The Vive suits a tall tub wall, and the Carex suits a bathroom where several people set the legs. The Medline benches are the pick if you want a padded seat, but we could not read Medline's own pages.</p>

<h2>The Bottom Line</h2>
<p>Choose by your tub: the Vive for a high wall, the Drive for the biggest seat, the Carex for easy adjustment, the Medline for a padded seat. Check the weight limit on the label, set the legs level before you sit and add grab bars as MedlinePlus advises. See also our guide to <a href="/best-shower-chairs/">shower chairs</a>.</p>'''

